Through this skill, I developed an understanding of the Linux development environment and the way the operating system manages processes and system-level operations. I learned how to work with a Linux virtual machine and configure the GCC compiler for compiling and executing C programs. I also learned how to create and organize a Git repository with a proper project structure so that source files, executables, and other project components can be managed systematically. Along with this, I gained an understanding of the basic architecture of the Linux shell, including how commands entered by a user are interpreted and executed. I also learned the purpose of a Makefile and how it can be used to automate the compilation of programs instead of manually entering compilation commands every time.
A major part of this skill was understanding the concept of process abstraction in Linux. I learned that when a program is executed, the operating system creates a process and provides it with resources such as CPU time, memory, and other required system resources. The process is managed by the Linux kernel and has important attributes such as a Process ID (PID), parent Process ID (PPID), state, and resource information. I understood that the operating system uses process abstraction to hide the complexity of the underlying hardware and provides programs with a controlled environment in which they can execute. This makes it possible for multiple programs to run concurrently while the operating system manages their resources.
I also learned about the fork() system call, which is used to create a new process. When fork() is called, the existing process becomes the parent and a new child process is created. The child initially receives a copy of the parent's process state, but both processes then execute independently. I learned how the return value of fork() can be used to distinguish between the parent and child processes. This helped me understand the basic concept of process creation and the parent-child relationship in Linux. I also learned that processes can be arranged in a hierarchical structure, where one process can create another process, resulting in a process tree.
Another important concept I learned was the exec() family of system calls. I understood that while fork() creates a new process, exec() replaces the program currently running inside a process with another program. This distinction is important in Linux because a common method of executing a command is for a shell or parent process to create a child using fork() and then allow the child to execute the required command using one of the exec() functions. I also learned about different functions in the exec() family, such as execl(), execlp(), execv(), and execvp(), and understood that they provide different ways of passing the program name and its arguments.
I further developed the skill of understanding and inspecting parent-child process relationships. By examining process IDs and process trees, I could understand how processes are connected and how the operating system keeps track of them. I learned that the parent process can use the wait() system call to wait for the termination of a child process. This prevents the parent from finishing before the child and also allows the parent to obtain information about the child's termination. Understanding this relationship helped me connect process creation, execution, synchronization, and termination as different stages of process management.
Finally, I learned the importance of system calls in communication between user programs and the Linux kernel. I used system call tracing concepts and tools such as strace to understand what happens internally when a program or command is executed. System calls provide controlled access to kernel services such as process creation, file operations, memory management, and program execution. By tracing system calls, I could observe that a simple command entered in the terminal can involve several interactions between the user-level program and the kernel. Overall, this skill helped me understand the Linux process model, process creation using fork(), program execution using the exec() family, parent-child synchronization, process hierarchy, shell operation, and the role of system calls in connecting user applications with the operating system kernel.
